// Static-analysis diagnostic codes.
//
// Subset 1 (meta/SUBSET_1.md). Cycle 0.5.1.
//
// Centralised for the reason 0.1.6, 0.2.1, 0.3.1 and 0.4.1 give: a mistyped code
// literal at a call site silently invents a NEW code, and the test asserting the
// old one then fails for a reason unrelated to the bug it guards.
//
// ONE PREFIX PER ANALYSIS, not one for the cycle. `NITPICK-BORROW` is D-004's
// escape rules; definite assignment, `move`, exhaustiveness, taint and lock levels
// each get their own as they land. A reader filtering a build log is asking "which
// analysis refused this", and a single `NITPICK-ANALYSIS` prefix would answer
// "one of six".

// --- D-004: second-class borrows (0.5.1) -------------------------------------
//
// FIVE RULES, FIVE CODES, plus the two situations that are not a rule violation.
// The plan asks for a diagnostic "naming which one broke", and a shared code
// would leave that to the message text — which the harness deliberately does not
// read (BUILD_REFERENCE 7.1), so a shared code is a distinction the tests could
// not assert on.

// Rule 2 — a borrow in the value of `pass`, `fail`, or `return`.
pub func:BORROW_RETURNED = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-001"; };

// Rule 3 — a borrow stored into something not provably shorter-lived.
pub func:BORROW_STORED = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-002"; };

// Rule 4, first crossing — a borrow passed to an `extern` function. Its own code
// and not a shared "rule 4", because this is the one that can fire today and the
// one whose reason is different in kind: the callee is not Nitpick, so nothing
// downstream can be checked at all.
// NITPICK-BORROW-003 lived here: D-004 rule 4, a borrow crossing an
// `extern` call. D-190 (1.1.13c) made the subject UNREPRESENTABLE — extern
// methods are an interface record whose generated stubs are ordinary
// Nitpick, nothing address-shaped can be declared in a block
// (NITPICK-EXTERN-001 is the enforcement, at the block, before names
// bind) — so the code is RETIRED. The number is not reused.

// Rule 4, second crossing — a borrow carried into a spawned thread.
pub func:BORROW_SPAWNED = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-004"; };

// Rule 4, third crossing — a borrow held across an `await`. Cycle 1.1 lowers
// `async`; the rule exists now because adding it after the analysis is verified
// is a re-verification nobody can afford.
pub func:BORROW_SUSPENDED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-005"; };

// Rule 5 — a closure capturing a borrow. Closures are removed (D-018), so this
// cannot fire; see `escape.npk` for why it is written anyway.
pub func:BORROW_CAPTURED = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-006"; };

// NOT A RULE VIOLATION — the analysis could not decide. A derivation chain deeper
// than `BORROW_FUEL` is refused rather than guessed at, and it says so in its own
// words instead of being reported as one of the five rules. A reader who sees this
// has written something the checker cannot classify, which is a different problem
// from having written an escape.
pub func:BORROW_TOO_DEEP = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-007"; };

// A DEFECT IN THE COMPILER, not in the program: an expression kind with no entry
// in `expr_shape`, so the walk cannot reach its children and cannot promise it
// looked everywhere. `RESOLVE_INTERNAL` and `TYPE_INTERNAL` exist for the same
// situation and for the same reason — a reader who sees it has found a hole in an
// analysis and there is nothing they can change in their source to fix it.
pub func:BORROW_INTERNAL = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-008"; };

// ALSO NOT A RULE VIOLATION — a borrow reaches a binding this analysis does not
// track, so it is refused rather than followed.
//
// Three kinds of binding exist and only one of them is tracked. A local
// (`SYM_STMT`) records whether it holds a borrow; a `pick` pattern binding
// (`SYM_PAT`) and a `for` loop binding do not, because both take their value from
// a destructuring the analysis would have to model to say anything about. Silently
// answering "not a borrow" for those two would be an escape hatch, so a borrow
// reaching either is refused with a sentence that says which.
pub func:BORROW_UNTRACKED = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-009"; };

// THE MARKING FIXPOINT DID NOT SETTLE within its round bound, so the analysis does
// not know which bindings hold a borrow and refuses to guess (D-116).
//
// ITS OWN CODE RATHER THAN `BORROW_INTERNAL`, though it is nearly always a defect
// in this compiler when it fires. The two are bounded by different things — one is
// a walk with no entry for a node kind, the other is a chain of borrow assignments
// longer than the rounds allow — and a shared code would make them one finding to
// anything reading a build log, which is the "distinction that lives only in the
// wording" cycle 0.4 kept paying for.
pub func:BORROW_UNSETTLED = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-BORROW-010"; };

// --- D-010: definite assignment (0.5.2) ---------------------------------------
//
// D-010 chose "no implicit default" over poisoning every scalar with `ERR`, on the
// argument that a default makes an uninitialised read DEFINED whereas the analysis
// makes it IMPOSSIBLE. These codes are that analysis reporting.

// A read of a binding that is not assigned on every path reaching it.
pub func:ASSIGN_UNASSIGNED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-ASSIGN-001"; };

// A second assignment to a `fixed` binding — local, parameter, struct field or
// module binding, which are one rule in four positions. One code for
// both, because it is one rule — assigned once, where it is declared — and a
// reader filtering a log is asking the same question either way.
pub func:ASSIGN_IMMUTABLE = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-ASSIGN-002"; };

// NOT A RULE VIOLATION — the walk hit its depth bound. Refused rather than
// assumed assigned, because "./we stopped looking" and "it is assigned" are the two
// answers this analysis exists to keep apart.
pub func:ASSIGN_TOO_DEEP = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-ASSIGN-003"; };

// A DEFECT IN THE COMPILER: a statement or expression kind the walk has no entry
// for, so it cannot promise it looked everywhere.
pub func:ASSIGN_INTERNAL = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-ASSIGN-004"; };

// --- D-065: `move`, and what a moved-from binding is (0.5.3) --------------------
//
// A moved-from binding is INVALID, not "valid but unspecified". D-065 is explicit
// that there is no such thing in this language as a value you may hold and not
// inspect, which is the `UNDEFINED_STATE_PREVENTION` discipline applied to
// ownership.

// A read of a binding that was moved out of.
pub func:MOVE_USE_AFTER = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-MOVE-001"; };

// A read of a binding whose allocation was handed to a deallocator. ITS OWN CODE
// rather than sharing with the above, because D-065 requires both sentences — the
// prototype's negative test asks for "use after move" AND "cannot free moved
// variable" — and a reader who sees this one has a dangling pointer rather than a
// transferred value.
pub func:MOVE_USE_AFTER_FREE = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-MOVE-002"; };

// `nodrop` on a binding that is not `wild` or `wildx`. The qualifier suppresses
// the drop of a manual allocation, which is the one case where not dropping is
// correct rather than a leak; on anything else it suppresses nothing and says
// something untrue about the binding it is written on.
pub func:MOVE_NODROP_MANAGED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-MOVE-003"; };

// D-183: `move` on a declaration that is not a PARAMETER. It states that the
// binding's ownership was transferred in by a caller, and only a parameter has
// a caller. On a local, a field or a global it is the same kind of untruth
// `nodrop` on a managed binding is — a reader who believes it is reasoning
// about a transfer that never happened.
pub func:MOVE_QUAL_NOT_PARAM = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-MOVE-004"; };

// --- exhaustiveness (0.5.4) ----------------------------------------------------
//
// A `pick` covers every case, and `(*)` may not absorb ERR (D-008 §5.1).

// A case the arms do not cover. The message NAMES the missing variants, because
// "this `pick` is not exhaustive" makes the reader enumerate the type themselves —
// the one thing the compiler already did.
pub func:PICK_NOT_EXHAUSTIVE = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-PICK-001"; };

// A `tbb` selector with no explicit `ERR:` arm. ITS OWN CODE rather than sharing
// with the above: a `pick` can have `(*)` and still be missing this, and reporting
// "not exhaustive" would send the author to add the wildcard they already wrote.
pub func:PICK_NO_ERR_ARM = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-PICK-002"; };

// A selector whose values cannot be listed — an integer, a float, a `char`. ITS
// OWN CODE because the fix differs: an enum with a variant missing needs an arm,
// and this needs a wildcard. A reader told "not exhaustive" about an `int32` goes
// looking for the variant they forgot.
pub func:PICK_NEEDS_WILDCARD = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-PICK-003"; };

// An arm nothing can reach: after an unguarded `(*)`, or naming a variant an
// earlier arm already matched. One code for both, because it is one mistake —
// something earlier already matches everything this arm could.
pub func:PICK_UNREACHABLE_ARM = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-PICK-004"; };

// A DEFECT IN THE COMPILER: a node kind the walk has no entry for, so it cannot
// promise it found every `pick`.
pub func:PICK_INTERNAL = string() never fails        { pass "NITPICK-PICK-005"; };

// --- D-007: the `unknown` taint on `Result.value` (0.5.5) -----------------------

// A read of `.value` on a `Result` nothing has checked for an error. `unknown` is
// compiler-assigned and not user-writable (`TYPE_REFERENCE` §27), so this is the
// only place the taint arises and the only place it is reported.
pub func:TAINT_UNCHECKED_VALUE = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-TAINT-001"; };

// --- D-056: lock levels (0.5.6) -------------------------------------------------
//
// `--verify-concurrency` claims data-race freedom and LOCK-ORDER freedom, and not
// deadlock freedom outright — the deadline backstop is containment, not proof. The
// flag saying what it proves is the point of the whole decision.

// A downward acquisition: holding level N and able to reach a level <= N. The
// message names BOTH levels, because "./acquisition must increase" without them makes
// the reader work out which two locks are involved.
pub func:LOCK_ORDER = string() never fails          { pass "NITPICK-LOCK-001"; };

// An implementation that can reach above the bound its trait method declared. Its
// own code: the fix is to lower what the implementation acquires or raise the
// declared bound, and neither is what a reader of an ordering error would try.
pub func:LOCK_EXCEEDS_BOUND = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-LOCK-002"; };

// An `acquires` level that is not a constant. Shares nothing with `TYPE_NOT_CONSTANT`
// because the two are found at different stages and a reader filtering a log is
// asking a different question — but the rule is deliberately the same, and since
// 0.6.4 the same EVALUATOR answers it: `acquires LOW` and `acquires BASE + 1i32`
// fold exactly as an array size does.
pub func:LOCK_NOT_CONSTANT = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-LOCK-003"; };

// A DEFECT IN THE COMPILER, or a program the walk could not follow: a node kind
// with no entry, or rounds that did not settle.
pub func:LOCK_INTERNAL = string() never fails       { pass "NITPICK-LOCK-004"; };

// The wildx W^X state machine (D-035/D-155, 0.10.5). Write after seal, and
// execute before seal, are the two transitions the seal state forbids; the
// rest (seal-after-free, double-free, leak) fall out of the move machinery
// and the exit-time registry.
pub func:WILDX_WRITE_SEALED = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-WILDX-001"; };
pub func:WILDX_EXEC_UNSEALED = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-WILDX-002"; };
// `--extra-picky=no-wildx` — a high-assurance build mode that excludes
// runtime code generation entirely (D-035's certification note). Separate
// from `no-wild`: manual memory and executable memory are different risks.
pub func:WILDX_EXCLUDED = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-WILDX-003"; };

// --- the suspend walk (D-177, 1.1.4) ---------------------------------------------
//
// The liveness-across-await walk records which locals cross a suspension; it
// refuses nothing, so its one code is the fail-closed internal defect every
// walk in this compiler carries: a construct the walk has no entry for means
// "nobody measured", and silence and "nothing to measure" must stay apart.
pub func:SUSPEND_INTERNAL = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-SUSPEND-001"; };

// D-178: a statement suspends at most once, before anything else it computes.
// The one real refusal the suspend walk owns — everything the statement
// evaluates before its `await` must be a bare literal (the await's own
// arguments are exempt: they are consumed into the callee's frame), an
// assignment whose value awaits takes a bare name as its target, and a
// second suspension in one statement is a second re-entry the first's
// values cannot survive.
pub func:SUSPEND_FIRST_EVAL = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-SUSPEND-002"; };

// An `await` inside a `pick` arm's `where` guard (1.1.13-close, D-191). A
// guard runs BETWEEN arm selection and arm entry, and its value cannot
// survive a suspension there — the arm bodies are frame-resident, the
// selection machinery is not, by design.
pub func:SUSPEND_GUARD = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-SUSPEND-003"; };

// NITPICK-SUSPEND-004 was drafted here for the indirect/builtin await and
// RETIRED UNUSED the same day (1.1.13-close): TYPE-043 already refuses an
// await of any non-async call, a SYNC function value is exactly that, and
// an ASYNC function VALUE is unspellable (the func-type grammar carries no
// `async`) — so the case has no program to arise in, and the backend's
// belt (iv_broken) covers the impossible remainder. The number is not
// reused.

// --- the reachable-error analysis (D-179, 1.1.7) ---------------------------------
//
// The program-wide set of errors that can reach `failsafe`, and the rule
// that its `pick` NAMES every member. `(*)` is permitted and counts for
// nothing — the compile-time force and the defensive floor, both.
pub func:REACH_NO_PICK = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-REACH-001"; };
pub func:REACH_UNNAMED = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-REACH-002"; };
